  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 PM Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Waves of diurnally-maximized convection, especially \nSun-Mon, will accompany a slow-moving mid/upr-level trough and \npreceding, deeply moist sswly flow through the middle of next week. \n\nA positively-tilted mid/upper trough will approach from the OH/TN \nValleys this weekend. Preceding sswly, and deep moisture \ncharacterized by PW values of 2-2.5 inches and 125-150% of normal, \nwill overspread cntl NC Sun-Mon, then probably linger especially \nover ern zones through mid-week. Despite widespread cloud cover, \nhigh temperatures in the 80s and dew points in the 70s should still \nallow for weak to moderate instability to develop. And by Monday, \nembedded upper impulses and possible upper divergence from a jet \nover the Appalachians may provide some forcing for ascent. All of \nthese ingredients should result in waves of showers and storms as \nearly as Sun morning over the wrn Piedmont and Sun afternoon through \nat least Mon-Mon night otherwise and elsewhere. The pattern will \nfavor marginal risks of both excessive rain and severe, especially \nSun-Mon. \n\nThe trough will lift NE and deamplify from Tuesday onward, but \ncontinued moist SW flow should result in above-normal shower and \nstorm chances through much of the workweek, albeit gradually \ndecreasing each day. This will keep temperatures in check with highs \nmostly in the 80s.",
